{"qid":"Q929","names":["Central African Republic"],"title":"Central African Republic","endonym":{"native":"Republique Centrafricaine","lang":"fr"},"capsule":"The Central African Republic declared independence from France on 13 August 1960 under President David Dacko, succeeding the territory of Oubangui-Chari in French Equatorial Africa. The country's post-independence history has been defined by chronic instability: Jean-Bédel Bokassa seized power in a coup in 1966, declared himself Emperor Bokassa I in December 1977, and was overthrown by French military intervention (Operation Barracuda) in September 1979; multiple coups followed. Since December 2012 a Séléka rebel coalition seized Bangui (March 2013), triggering sectarian violence between Séléka and Anti-balaka militias that displaced hundreds of thousands, prompting the deployment of the UN peacekeeping mission MINUSCA in 2014.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. National boundaries inherited from French colonial Oubangui-Chari are established by treaty and internationally recognized; effective governmental control of the interior has been fragmentary since 2013.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Central African Republic","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Central_African_Republic"},{"label":"Britannica: Central African Republic","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Central-African-Republic"},{"label":"UN MINUSCA mandate","url":"https://peacekeeping.un.org/en/mission/minusca"}],"britannica":"place/Central-African-Republic","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Central African Republic","rulers":[{"qid":"Q162708","name":"Jean-Bedel Bokassa","st